WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:04.460
A formula can look right, reference real cells, and still return the wrong answer.

2
00:00:04.801 --> 00:00:07.861
That’s why FormulaBench doesn’t stop at valid syntax.

3
00:00:08.202 --> 00:00:11.361
It asks whether every target cell is actually correct.

4
00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:19.886
The harness inspects the workbook, builds sheet-aware context, samples Qwen once, then validates exact coverage and formula safety.

5
00:00:20.299 --> 00:00:27.319
Only then does it write atomically, or fail closed, before the organiser-supplied evaluator scores the workbook.

6
00:00:27.712 --> 00:00:33.612
In our public self-evaluation of four hundred benchmark tasks, one hundred thirty-three passed:

7
00:00:33.993 --> 00:00:39.953
a thirty-three point two five percent pass rate, with forty point five six percent cell accuracy.

8
00:00:40.474 --> 00:00:44.274
There were zero missing predictions and zero evaluator errors.

9
00:00:44.814 --> 00:00:50.794
But ninety-four point eight four percent of wrong cells sat inside contract-valid workbooks.

10
00:00:51.734 --> 00:00:53.514
Running is not the same as right.

11
00:00:53.965 --> 00:01:02.906
The next step is targeted: deterministic transformations where rules are reliable, plus formula-execution checks where semantics decide the answer.
